#Understanding Bubble Mart (1): 3 common misconceptions about Bubble Mart

In the past three years, Bubble Mart’s profits have increased ninefold.

It’s not a nine-fold increase in revenue, it’s a net profit—from 1.4 billion to 12.8 billion.

Open the 2025 annual report, and you'll see a set of numbers that don't look like a consumer products company:

| Indicators | 2025 | What level |
|------|--------|---------|
| Revenue | 37.12 billion (+185%) | Extremely rare growth rate in the consumer goods industry |
| Net profit | 12.8 billion (+309%) | Only 1.4 billion 3 years ago |
| Gross profit margin | 72.1% | Exceeding Hermès |
| ROE | 55% | Printing press level |
| Net cash | 13.8 billion | Zero debt |

72% gross profit margin. ROE of 55%. There is 13.8 billion in cash on the account, and there is not a penny of debt.

Putting this set of figures into any industry is enough to make analysts excitedly write a 50-page in-depth report.

But how many times has the market valued it?

13 to 15 times.

Sanrio, which also does IP business, has a 38 times increase.

If you think this is a "seriously underestimated money printing machine" - you probably don't understand.

If you think this is "the fashion bubble is about to burst" - you probably don't understand.

The real situation of Bubble Mart is more complicated than both of these judgments. It has all the financial characteristics of a top-tier business, plus the risk signals that keep people awake at night. However, most people's understanding of it still remains in three outdated illusions.

---

## Illusion 1: Bubble Mart is a blind box company

This is the most widespread and outdated label.

When it goes public in 2020, "the first blind box stock" is indeed the loudest selling point. Drawing blind boxes and speculating on hidden items, this set of gameplay made Bubble Mart out of the circle.

But five years later, blind boxes only account for a small part of Bubble Mart’s revenue.

Today, Bubble Mart sells action figures, plush toys, building blocks, opens a theme park, licenses IP, and is even about to co-produce a movie with Sony Pictures - hiring the director of "Paddington Bear".

What it is really doing is IP operations.

Specifically: incubate or sign a batch of trendy images yourself, and then develop as many product forms and consumption scenarios as possible around these images. Blind boxes are just one of the carriers, and they are becoming less and less important.

Let’s make an analogy: saying that Bubble Mart is a blind box company is like saying that Disney is an animation company. Cartoons are the starting point, but Disney’s real business is to build a complete monetization system around IP—parks, peripherals, licensing, and streaming media.

Bubble Mart is following the same path. But its starting point is not cartoons, but trendy toys.

Why is this distinction important?

Because "blind box company" means that the ceiling is very low - blind box is such a big market, and it will be over as soon as the popularity is over. But the ceiling of the "IP operation platform" depends on the vitality of the IP itself and the width of the monetization path. These are two completely different valuation logics.

Of course, whether we can really take the path of Disney is another question. But at least you have to put it into the right analytical framework first.

---

## Illusion 2: 72% gross profit margin = huge profits = good business

When I saw the 72% gross profit margin, my first reaction was: This is too profitable.

Make money or not? Of course you make money. The net profit of 12.8 billion is there and does not need to be discussed.

But there is a key question between "making money" and "good business": How long can this money be earned? **

Where does Bubble Mart’s high gross profit come from? The raw materials are plastic and cloth, and the cost is extremely low. The premium consumers pay is essentially paying for their emotions - they find the image cute, want to own it, and are willing to spend dozens to hundreds of dollars to satisfy this emotional need.

This is essentially different from Hermès’ high gross profit.

Behind Hermès' 72% gross profit margin is the brand potential accumulated over more than 100 years, artificial scarcity, and the social currency attribute of "the more expensive it is, the more you want to buy it." Its moat was built over time.

Behind Bubble Mart’s 72% gross profit margin is a rabbit named LABUBU.

In 2025, LABUBU’s IP contributed 38.1% of the company’s revenue. Nearly 40%.

One IP supports 40% of the business of a company with a revenue of 37.1 billion. You can say that it proves Bubble Mart’s ability to create popular products, but you must also admit: this revenue structure is extremely concentrated.

If LABUBU becomes popular, its gross profit margin will be high and its growth will be rapid. What if LABUBU stops being popular one day?

Some people will say: Bubble Mart is not only LABUBU, but also Molly, Dimoo, Ono, Hirono... That's right. But the data does not lie: one IP accounts for 40% of the revenue, which shows that the individual contributions of other IPs are far from the same magnitude.

This is the core contradiction of the emotional premium business - the gross profit margin is high precisely because the things you sell have no functional needs, and consumers can stop buying them at any time. ** What you make is money from emotions, and emotions come and go quickly. No one can predict how long the popularity of a trendy IP will last.

Therefore, a 72% gross profit margin alone cannot tell you whether this is a good business. You have to answer the question "how long will it last?" And this question, frankly speaking, no one can answer. Including Bubble Mart himself.

