## Illusion 3: 13 times P/E ratio is cheap

A P/E ratio of 13 to 15 times, compared to Sanrio’s 38 times, looks like a bargain.

But this comparison has a fatal premise: you assume that the current profit level can be maintained.

Let's do some math.

The profit in 2025 will be 12.8 billion, with a growth rate of 309%. This growth rate is obviously unsustainable - the company's own revenue guidance for 2026 is only about 20% growth.

Interestingly, the actual growth rate in the first quarter of 2026 reached 75% to 80%, far exceeding the guidance. Management is either conservative or unsure whether it can sustain it in the next few quarters.

In either case, from 185% to 20% (or even to 75%), the direction of growth is downward. The question is just where to stabilize it.

If the growth rate drops to 20% next year and 10% the year after that, based on the current profit base of 12.8 billion, the profit will be about 17 billion in two years. The market value currently given by the market is approximately 160 to 190 billion.

A profit of 17 billion corresponds to a market value of 190 billion, almost 11 times.

A price-earnings ratio of 11 times and a growth rate of 10% - this is not cheap, it is reasonable or even expensive.

What if the growth rate drops to single digits? What if the popularity of LABUBU fades and the next hot IP cannot be picked up? That 13 times is not the bottom, but the starting point.

Let’s look at another set of sobering data:

| Signals | Numbers | What they mean |
|------|------|-----------|
| Inventory growth | +259% | Faster than revenue growth, either aggressive stocking or unable to sell |
| Management reduces holdings | HK$1.7 billion | People who know the company best are selling |
| LABUBU revenue share | 38.1% | Highly dependent on a single IP |

Inventory growth was 259%, faster than revenue growth of 185%. This means that the amount of goods the company is stocking is growing faster than the amount of goods it is selling. It may be that you are confident about the future and stock up in advance. It may also be that the sales of some products are not as good as expected. In the consumer goods industry, inventory growth consistently outpaces revenue growth, which is generally not a good sign.

Management reduced its holdings by HK$1.7 billion. There are many reasons for executives to sell their own stocks - to improve their lives, asset allocation, personal spending, and it doesn't necessarily mean they are bearish. But when a group of executives sell out en masse at the peak of their company's performance, you should at least ask: What did they see?

